 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Shark NZ801UK anti hair wrap upright - suction has gone really weak over the last couple of weeks, barely picks up crumbs. Have emptied the bin. Any ideas before I buy a new one?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "It is very unlikely you need a new vacuum yet; weak suction is almost always caused by restricted airflow rather than a broken motor. The next most common cause on this model is dirty or damp filters. Please check if the pre-motor and HEPA filters are clean and, crucially, whether they were left to dry completely for 24 hours after washing, as a damp filter will choke suction just like a full one.\n[Helpful to see: image \"Poor suction — what to check first\" — Lost suction is usually a full bin, a dirty filter or a blockage in the hose, tube or floor head — check and clear those before suspecting the motor. Switch off and unplug first. Typical layout — your model may differ.]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I've never washed them, didn't know they were there. The foam one is solid grey and the felt one is caked with fine dust. No burning smell or overheating, just a dusty smell from the exhaust. What should I do with them?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Those filters are most likely choking the suction; fine dust blocks the pores just as effectively as hair or lint. You need to wash them in cold water only (no soap), shake off the excess, and leave them to dry completely for at least 24 hours. Do not use the vacuum until they are bone-dry, as a damp filter will still restrict airflow significantly. Once they are fully dry, reassemble and test it."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Ok, washed both in lukewarm water and left them 24 hours to dry. Massive difference, suction is back to like new. Thanks."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "That is excellent news; it suggests the filters were simply choked with fine dust. To stop this happening again, try to wash and dry them every couple of months or whenever you notice suction dropping slightly. If anything else comes up with the vacuum, just let me know."
  }
 ],
